  "evaluation": "All three correctly classify the disagreement as genuine, prioritize population differences, and identify valid secondary differences. B is strongest because it clearly ties the population contrast to the reported enrollment fields and explicitly distinguishes the comparators and instruments. A is similarly sound, though less explicit about the comparator difference. C is also largely correct, but its description of Study B's enrolled population as having high depression scores risks treating reported follow-up scores as an enrollment or baseline characteristic.",
